 as FwpmCalloutCreateEnumHandle0, have specific functions to release memory.\nL57: \nL58: If the caller passes a pointer that is not valid, the behavior is undefined.\nL59: \nL60: FwpmFreeMemory0 is a specific implementation of FwpmFreeMemory. See cite5†WFP Version-Independent Names and Targeting Specific Versions of Windows for more information.\nL61: ## Requirements\nL62: \nL63: Requirement  | Value\nL64: --- | ---\nL65: Minimum supported client  | Windows Vista [desktop apps only]\nL66: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2008 [desktop apps only]\nL67: Target Platform  | Windows\nL68: Header  | fwpmu.h\nL69: Library  | Fwpuclnt.lib\nL70: DLL  | Fwpuclnt.dll\nL71: \nL72: ## See also\nL73: \nL74: cite6†WFP Functions L75: \nL76: cite7†Windows Filtering Platform API Reference L77: \nL78: * * *\nL79: ## Feedback\nL80: \nL81: Was this page helpful?\nL82: \nL83: Yes No No\nL84: \nL85: Need help with this topic?\nL86: \nL87: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL88: \nL89: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL90: \nL91: Suggest a fix?\nL92: \nL93: * * *\nL94: \nL95: ## Additional resources\nL96: \nL97: * * *\nL98: \nL99:   * Last updated on 2024-02-22\nL100: \nL101: ### In this article\nL102: \nL103: Was this page helpful?\nL104: \nL105: Need help with this topic?\nL106: \nL107: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL108: \nL109: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL110: \nL111: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCloseServiceHandle function (winsvc.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winsvc/nf-winsvc-closeservicehandle)\nciteturn308view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winsvc/nf-winsvc-closeservicehandle\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 124\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # CloseServiceHandle function (winsvc.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Closes a handle to a service control manager or service object.\nL33: \nL34: ## Syntax\nL35: \nL36:     `BOOL CloseServiceHandle(\nL37:       [in] SC_HANDLE hSCObject\nL38:     );\nL39:     `\nL40: ## Parameters\nL41: \nL42: `[in] hSCObject`\nL43: \nL44: A handle to the service control manager object or the service object to close. Handles to service control manager objects are returned by the cite5†OpenSCManager function, and handles to service objects are returned by either the cite6†OpenService or cite7†CreateService function.\nL45: ## Return value\nL46: \nL47: If the function succeeds, the return value is nonzero.\nL48: \nL49: If the function fails, the return value is zero. To get extended error information, call cite8†GetLastError .\nL50: \nL51: The following error code can be set by the service control manager. Other error codes can be set by registry functions that are called by the service control manager.\nL52: \nL53: Return code  | Description\nL54: --- | ---\nL55: \nL56: ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE\nL57:  | The specified handle is invalid.\nL58: ## Remarks\nL59: \nL60: The CloseServiceHandle function does not destroy the service control manager object referred to by the handle. A service control manager object cannot be destroyed. A service object can be destroyed by calling the cite9†DeleteService function.\nL61: \nL62: #### Examples\nL63: \nL64: For an example, see cite10†Deleting a Service .\nL65: ## Requirements\nL66: \nL67: Requirement  | Value\nL68: --- | ---\nL69: Minimum supported client  | Windows XP [desktop apps only]\nL70: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps only]\nL71: Target Platform  | Windows\nL72: Header  | winsvc.h (include Windows.h)\nL73: Library  | Advapi32.lib\nL74: DLL  | Advapi32.dll\nL75: \nL76: ## See also\nL77: \nL78: cite7†CreateService L79: \nL80: cite9†DeleteService L81: \nL82: cite5†OpenSCManager L83: \nL84: cite6†OpenService L85: \nL86: cite11†SCM Handles L87: \nL88: cite12†Service Functions L89: \nL90: * * *\nL91: ## Feedback\nL92: \nL93: Was this page helpful?\nL94: \nL95: Yes No No\nL96: \nL97: Need help with this topic?\nL98: \nL99: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL100: \nL101: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL102: \nL103: Suggest a fix?\nL104: \nL105: * * *\nL106: \nL107: ## Additional resources\nL108: \nL109: * * *\nL110: \nL111:   * Last updated on 2021-10-13\nL112: \nL113: ### In this article\nL114: \nL115: Was this page helpful?\nL116: \nL117: Need help with this topic?\nL118: \nL119: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL120: \nL121: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL122: \nL123: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocalFree function (winbase.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-localfree)\nciteturn308view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-localfree\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 125\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # LocalFree function (winbase.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Frees the specified local memory object and invalidates its handle.\nL33: \nL34: Note  The local functions have greater overhead and provide fewer features than other memory management functions. New applications should use the cite5†heap functions unless documentation states that a local function should be used. For more information, see cite6†Global and Local Functions .\nL35: ## Syntax\nL36: \nL37:     `HLOCAL LocalFree(\nL38:       [in] _Frees_ptr_opt_ HLOCAL hMem\nL39:     );\nL40:     `\nL41: \nL42: ## Parameters\nL43: \nL44: `[in] hMem`\nL45: \nL46: A handle to the local memory object. This handle is returned by either the cite7†LocalAlloc or cite8†LocalReAlloc function. It is not safe to free memory allocated with cite9†GlobalAlloc .\nL47: ## Return value\nL48: \nL49: If the function succeeds, the return value is NULL.\nL50: \nL51: If the function fails, the return value is equal to a handle to the local memory object. To get extended error information, call cite10†GetLastError .\nL52: ## Remarks\nL53: \nL54: If the process tries to examine or modify the memory after it has been freed, heap corruption may occur or an access violation exception (EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION) may be generated.\nL55: \nL56: If the hMem parameter is NULL, LocalFree ignores the parameter and returns NULL.\nL57: The LocalFree function will free a locked memory object. A locked memory object has a lock count greater than zero. The cite11†LocalLock function locks a local memory object and increments the lock count by one. The cite12†LocalUnlock function unlocks it and decrements the lock count by one. To get the lock count of a local memory object, use the cite13†LocalFlags function.\nL58: If an application is running under a debug version of the system, LocalFree will issue a message that tells you that a locked object is being freed. If you are debugging the application, LocalFree will enter a breakpoint just before freeing a locked object. This allows you to verify the intended behavior, then continue execution.\nL59: #### Examples\nL60: \nL61: For an example, see cite7†LocalAlloc .\nL62: \nL63: ## Requirements\nL64: \nL65: Requirement  | Value\nL66: --- | ---\nL67: Minimum supported client  | Windows XP [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL68: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL69: Target Platform  | Windows\nL70: Header  | winbase.h (include Windows.h)\nL71: Library  | Kernel32.lib\nL72: DLL  | Kernel32.dll\nL73: ## See also\nL74: \nL75: cite6†Global and Local Functions L76: \nL77: cite14†GlobalFree L78: \nL79: cite7†LocalAlloc L80: \nL81: cite13†LocalFlags L82: \nL83: cite11†LocalLock L84: \nL85: cite8†LocalReAlloc L86: \nL87: cite12†LocalUnlock L88: \nL89: cite15†Memory Management Functions L90: \nL91: * * *\nL92: \nL93: ## Feedback\nL94: \nL95: Was this page helpful?\nL96: \nL97: Yes No N