L29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Frees memory allocated by a Remote Desktop Services function.\nL33: \nL34: ## Syntax\nL35: \nL36:     `VOID WTSFreeMemory(\nL37:       [in] PVOID pMemory\nL38:     );\nL39:     `\nL40: \nL41: ## Parameters\nL42: \nL43: `[in] pMemory`\nL44: \nL45: Pointer to the memory to free.\nL46: \nL47: ## Return value\nL48: \nL49: None\nL50: \nL51: ## Remarks\nL52: \nL53: Several Remote Desktop Services functions allocate buffers to return information. Use the WTSFreeMemory function to free these buffers.\nL54: ## Requirements\nL55: \nL56: Requirement  | Value\nL57: --- | ---\nL58: Minimum supported client  | Windows Vista\nL59: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2008\nL60: Target Platform  | Windows\nL61: Header  | wtsapi32.h\nL62: Library  | Wtsapi32.lib\nL63: DLL  | Wtsapi32.dll\nL64: API set  | ext-ms-win-session-wtsapi32-l1-1-0 (introduced in Windows 8)\nL65: \nL66: ## See also\nL67: \nL68: cite5†WTSEnumerateProcesses L69: \nL70: cite6†WTSEnumerateSessions L71: \nL72: cite7†WTSQuerySessionInformation L73: \nL74: cite8†WTSQueryUserConfig L75: \nL76: * * *\nL77: ## Feedback\nL78: \nL79: Was this page helpful?\nL80: \nL81: Yes No No\nL82: \nL83: Need help with this topic?\nL84: \nL85: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL86: \nL87: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL88: \nL89: Suggest a fix?\nL90: \nL91: * * *\nL92: \nL93: ## Additional resources\nL94: \nL95: * * *\nL96: \nL97:   * Last updated on 2024-02-22\nL98: \nL99: ### In this article\nL100: \nL101: Was this page helpful?\nL102: \nL103: Need help with this topic?\nL104: \nL105: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL106: \nL107: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL108: \nL109: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommandLineToArgvW function (shellapi.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/shellapi/nf-shellapi-commandlinetoargvw)\nciteturn308view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/shellapi/nf-shellapi-commandlinetoargvw\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 152\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # CommandLineToArgvW function (shellapi.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Parses a Unicode command line string and returns an array of pointers to the command line arguments, along with a count of such arguments, in a way that is similar to the standard C run-time argv and argc values.\nL33: \nL34: ## Syntax\nL35: \nL36:     `LPWSTR * CommandLineToArgvW(\nL37:       [in]  LPCWSTR lpCmdLine,\nL38:       [out] int     *pNumArgs\nL39:     );\nL40:     `\nL41: ## Parameters\nL42: \nL43: `[in] lpCmdLine`\nL44: \nL45: Type: LPCWSTR\nL46: \nL47: Pointer to a null-terminated Unicode string that contains the full command line. If this parameter is an empty string the function returns the path to the current executable file.\nL48: \nL49: `[out] pNumArgs`\nL50: \nL51: Type: int*\nL52: \nL53: Pointer to an int that receives the number of array elements returned, similar to argc.\nL54: ## Return value\nL55: \nL56: Type: LPWSTR*\nL57: \nL58: A pointer to an array of LPWSTR values, similar to argv.\nL59: \nL60: If the function fails, the return value is NULL. To get extended error information, call cite5†GetLastError .\nL61: ## Remarks\nL62: \nL63: The address returned by CommandLineToArgvW is the address of the first element in an array of LPWSTR values; the number of pointers in this array is indicated by pNumArgs. Each pointer to a null-terminated Unicode string represents an individual argument found on the command line.\nL64: CommandLineToArgvW allocates a block of contiguous memory for pointers to the argument strings, and for the argument strings themselves; the calling application must free the memory used by the argument list when it is no longer needed. To free the memory, use a single call to the cite6†LocalFree function.\nL65: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFwpmGetAppIdFromFileName0 function (fwpmu.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fwpmu/nf-fwpmu-fwpmgetappidfromfilename0)\nciteturn308view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fwpmu/nf-fwpmu-fwpmgetappidfromfilename0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\n"
